4. I — Interface Segregation Principle (ISP)
Simple definition

Don't force a class to implement methods it doesn't need.

❌ Bad example

Suppose:

public interface Employee {


    void work();


    void eat();


    void sleep();
}

Now imagine a RobotEmployee.

public class RobotEmployee implements Employee {


    @Override
    public void work() {
        System.out.println("Working");
    }


    @Override
    public void eat() {
        // Robot doesn't eat!
    }


    @Override
    public void sleep() {
        // Robot doesn't sleep!
    }
}

We are forcing Robot to implement methods it doesn't need.

✅ Better

Create smaller interfaces:

public interface Workable {
    void work();
}
public interface Eatable {
    void eat();
}
public interface Sleepable {
    void sleep();
}

Human:

public class Human implements Workable, Eatable, Sleepable {


    public void work() {}


    public void eat() {}


    public void sleep() {}
}

Robot:

public class Robot implements Workable {


    public void work() {}
}

Robot only implements what it actually needs.

Spring Boot example

Instead of creating a huge interface:

public interface UserService {


    void createUser();


    void deleteUser();


    void sendEmail();


    void generateReport();


    void exportExcel();


    void processPayment();
}

You might separate responsibilities:

UserService
EmailService
ReportService
PaymentService

This makes the application easier to maintain.